WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:09.720
In the mouse cortex, the first neurons born end up deepest, beside the white matter.

2
00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:13.880
Neurons born days later end up near the surface.

3
00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:18.640
Yet these neurons are all made at the ventricle, below.

4
00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:24.400
So the later neurons must climb past every neuron made before them.

5
00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:28.780
How does a neuron's birthday decide where it settles?

6
00:00:29.633 --> 00:00:32.093
Progenitors line the ventricle.

7
00:00:32.693 --> 00:00:35.753
They divide, and the new neurons travel outward.

8
00:00:36.673 --> 00:00:41.733
The first to leave form a thin sheet under the outer surface, the preplate.

9
00:00:42.653 --> 00:00:47.093
The next wave settles inside the preplate and splits it in two.

10
00:00:48.013 --> 00:00:49.953
The outer part is the marginal zone.

11
00:00:50.673 --> 00:00:52.453
The inner part is the subplate.

12
00:00:53.593 --> 00:00:58.333
Each later wave passes the neurons already there and settles above them.

13
00:00:58.333 --> 00:01:00.673
Layer 6 is made first.

14
00:01:01.633 --> 00:01:03.113
Layer 2 is made last.

15
00:01:04.183 --> 00:01:06.783
How do you know when a neuron was born?

16
00:01:07.703 --> 00:01:11.483
You date its last S phase, when DNA is copied.

17
00:01:12.643 --> 00:01:18.363
Give a pregnant mouse one dose of thymidine carrying tritium, a radioactive form of

18
00:01:18.363 --> 00:01:24.083
hydrogen. Only cells copying DNA in the next hour or so are marked.

19
00:01:25.083 --> 00:01:27.823
A cell that divides again halves its label.

20
00:01:27.823 --> 00:01:33.903
So a neuron that stays heavily labeled made its last copy soon after the pulse.

21
00:01:34.563 --> 00:01:36.203
The label marks that moment.

22
00:01:36.763 --> 00:01:38.063
It tells the cell nothing.

23
00:01:39.017 --> 00:01:46.517
In 1961, Jay Angevine and Richard Sidman gave pulses on different embryonic days.

24
00:01:47.857 --> 00:01:49.897
Cells labeled earliest lay deepest.

25
00:01:51.057 --> 00:01:53.937
Cells labeled on later days lay nearer the surface.

26
00:01:55.077 --> 00:01:57.017
This ruled out a simple idea.

27
00:01:57.817 --> 00:02:03.557
If new cells just shoved older ones outward, the earliest cells would end up on top.

28
00:02:04.497 --> 00:02:05.737
They ended up on the bottom.

29
00:02:07.037 --> 00:02:08.157
Later cells passed them.

30
00:02:08.950 --> 00:02:14.630
Does the progenitor carry its own schedule, or do its surroundings tell its daughter

31
00:02:14.630 --> 00:02:20.810
where to go? Susan McConnell moved ferret progenitors that make deep-layer neurons

32
00:02:20.810 --> 00:02:22.390
into an older host.

33
00:02:23.710 --> 00:02:28.830
Cells moved while copying DNA mostly settled in the upper layers, like the host's

34
00:02:28.830 --> 00:02:33.990
own neurons. Cells moved later in the cycle mostly settled in the deep layers.

35
00:02:34.430 --> 00:02:36.070
Their fate was already fixed.

36
00:02:36.070 --> 00:02:41.170
Late progenitors put into a young host still made upper-layer neurons.

37
00:02:42.033 --> 00:02:45.653
So a neuron's birthday predicts its layer.

38
00:02:46.573 --> 00:02:48.253
Early neurons sit deep.

39
00:02:49.293 --> 00:02:51.533
Later neurons pass them and settle above.

40
00:02:52.433 --> 00:02:56.453
A progenitor's choices narrow with time, and they do not reverse.

41
00:02:57.333 --> 00:03:02.773
This is a strong tendency of the mammalian neocortex, with some overlap between

42
00:03:02.773 --> 00:03:04.773
days. Keep the rule.

43
00:03:05.493 --> 00:03:08.633
When a neuron is born predicts where it settles.
